WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.832
<v Policy Analyst>Do you have ten minutes? I need help with the

00:00:02.832 --> 00:00:05.664
<v Policy Analyst>next two weeks. A new data request came in this

00:00:05.664 --> 00:00:08.496
<v Policy Analyst>morning for Friday, and I do not think everything fits.

00:00:08.846 --> 00:00:11.857
<v Human Services Supervisor>Can you squeeze it in? It is for the director's

00:00:11.857 --> 00:00:12.158
<v Human Services Supervisor>meeting.

00:00:12.508 --> 00:00:15.940
<v Policy Analyst>That was my first instinct too. So I checked how

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:19.371
<v Policy Analyst>long similar work took last time instead of guessing. The

00:00:19.371 --> 00:00:22.803
<v Policy Analyst>last data summary took about 14 hours with reviews. The

00:00:22.803 --> 00:00:26.234
<v Policy Analyst>training presentation took 20. With the report section, the inbox

00:00:26.234 --> 00:00:29.666
<v Policy Analyst>and my two days of leave, it comes to about

00:00:29.666 --> 00:00:32.068
<v Policy Analyst>70 hours of work. I have 52.

00:00:32.418 --> 00:00:36.087
<v Human Services Supervisor>Seventy against fifty-two. I would have guessed it was tight,

00:00:36.087 --> 00:00:37.554
<v Human Services Supervisor>not that far over.

00:00:37.904 --> 00:00:41.777
<v Policy Analyst>Most people underestimate. In one study, students thought their theses

00:00:41.777 --> 00:00:45.650
<v Policy Analyst>would take about 34 days. They took about 55. Looking

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:49.136
<v Policy Analyst>at the last similar task is what fixes it.

00:00:49.486 --> 00:00:52.534
<v Human Services Supervisor>All right. Is Friday the real deadline?

00:00:52.884 --> 00:00:56.217
<v Policy Analyst>I asked. The meeting is next Thursday, so Friday was

00:00:56.217 --> 00:00:59.550
<v Policy Analyst>a buffer. That helps, but it still does not fit.

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:02.882
<v Policy Analyst>I have two options. I can deliver the summary Wednesday

00:01:02.882 --> 00:01:06.215
<v Policy Analyst>if inbox response times slow from two days to four

00:01:06.215 --> 00:01:09.548
<v Policy Analyst>this week. Or the data team's manager could assign it

00:01:09.548 --> 00:01:10.548
<v Policy Analyst>to another analyst.

00:01:10.898 --> 00:01:14.572
<v Human Services Supervisor>That is clear. I would rather not slow the inbox.

00:01:14.572 --> 00:01:18.247
<v Human Services Supervisor>Counties are waiting on answers about the policy change. Let

00:01:18.247 --> 00:01:21.554
<v Human Services Supervisor>me ask the data team's manager about another analyst.

00:01:21.904 --> 00:01:24.869
<v Policy Analyst>Thank you. Could I hear by tomorrow? If it stays

00:01:24.869 --> 00:01:26.944
<v Policy Analyst>with me, I need to start Thursday.

00:01:27.294 --> 00:01:30.993
<v Human Services Supervisor>Tomorrow morning. And I am noticing this is the third

00:01:30.993 --> 00:01:33.582
<v Human Services Supervisor>reporting cycle with a collision like this.

00:01:33.932 --> 00:01:37.785
<v Policy Analyst>It is. Two managers approve work separately, so nobody sees

00:01:37.785 --> 00:01:40.868
<v Policy Analyst>the total until it lands on one person.

00:01:41.218 --> 00:01:44.830
<v Human Services Supervisor>Then the fix is not you planning harder. I will

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:48.443
<v Human Services Supervisor>ask both managers to agree on one order of priority,

00:01:48.443 --> 00:01:52.055
<v Human Services Supervisor>or one person who decides when requests collide. Bring this

00:01:52.055 --> 00:01:55.306
<v Human Services Supervisor>picture to that conversation. It makes the choice visible.
